WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:12.169
Thank you, everybody, for joining us

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:13.669
today to go through this walkthrough

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.589
of the Foundry platform.

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:16.939
There's a lot to cover, so it'll be

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.079
a bit of a whirlwind tour.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.009
I won't be able to get to

00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.749
everything, but I'll try to give you

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.219
a representative cross sample of

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.239
kind of the key parts of the

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.679
platform.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.685
Foundry behind the scenes is 100+

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.669
different microservices kind

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.799
of surfaced up as kind of two dozen

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.508
different out-of-the-box user facing

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:34.429
applications, actually a bit more.

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:36.289
In addition to applications that you

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:38.149
can also build. And these

00:00:38.150 --> 00:00:39.559
applications stretch across

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.419
effectively every persona in

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.589
the modern enterprise.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:43.939
So we'll try to take a tour through

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:45.679
kind of some of those key personas

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.359
and call out what kind of tools or

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.189
services are used at every step

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:50.190
along the journey.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:51.859
You know, we can think about the

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.269
functionality and foundry kind of in

00:00:53.270 --> 00:00:55.249
three main parts.

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.109
So there is the data and ontology

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.819
piece. So how are we connecting to

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.469
different data sources, data lakes,

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.389
data warehouses and bringing that

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.219
data together into a semantic

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:06.079
foundation that can then be used by

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.639
the entire organization.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.169
We call this an ontology.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.549
So we'll talk about the journey of

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:11.839
how we connect into different data

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.759
sources, refine them, build

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.919
out data integrations with

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.659
health checks, security, and kind of

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:19.549
build out this ontology as

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:20.630
sort of a starting place

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.209
will then want to bring in kind of

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.069
data science and modeling into that

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:25.999
same foundation.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.529
So this is sort of the sphere of

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.209
digital twin and modeling.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:30.649
So how do we think about leveraging

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.929
business logic, data science,

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.909
ML, LP models

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:36.709
within the same foundation that

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:38.419
we've built on the data side?

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:40.369
So kind of trying to fuze data

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.499
and models together into a common

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:43.916
foundation through this ontology.

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:46.609
And then kind of finally, we'll talk

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:48.379
about how the combination of data

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:50.269
and models comes together

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.249
for different types of end users.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:53.599
So, you know, these are everyday

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.189
users doing core operational

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.049
functions in the organization,

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.209
and this is 70 to 80 percent

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:01.519
of users of Foundry in most contexts

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:02.419
are sort of quote unquote

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.279
non-technical users trying to

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:05.989
make use of the fruits of all that

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:08.359
labor towards real operational

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.189
workflows and different

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:11.479
applications that are kind of

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.369
existential to the business.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.199
So let's start at the

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.059
top with data and ontology.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:18.289
So what I want to do is first go

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.179
into a first application

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:21.829
here, which is called Monocle, which

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:23.339
is sort of our data lineage graph

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.099
for the story of kind of how the

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:26.419
data comes together into an

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:27.409
ontology.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.759
So here you'll see this is a pretty

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:29.539
notional example.

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.189
So it's a little bit simplified a

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:33.319
supply chain ontology, right, which

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.269
is showing me kind of the

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.709
different concepts I'm going to work

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.779
with in my supply chain demo.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.639
So I've put together common

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.619
concepts, right? Like a supplier, an

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:45.319
alert, a distribution center,

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.329
plants, materials, et cetera.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:49.249
And each of these also have each

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.809
of these objects have links between

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:51.919
them. We'll go into how each of

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:53.419
these objects also has actions

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.949
associated with them.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:56.719
But the idea is, is that if we look

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.279
at kind of any one of these objects,

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.329
so we're going to go into the plant

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:02.029
to begin with here to start.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.559
Each of these is the culmination of

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:04.969
many different steps of data

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:06.019
integration.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.669
So if we zoom out here, what we see

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.559
kind of in this spiderweb diagram

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.419
is the story of how we integrated

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.189
different data sources to build this

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:14.899
plant object to start with on the

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.249
right hand side.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:18.259
So reading this from left to right,

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.459
what we see here is we're actually

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.079
pulling in kind of a couple

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.099
different data sources.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:23.569
Typically, these diagrams are much,

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:24.769
much larger, much, much more

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.509
complex. But this is just a starting

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:28.309
example of us connecting to.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.289
You'll see on the left hand side

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.599
and SAP system as an example

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:34.279
of one business system,

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.229
Treasury.gov, which is an example

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.249
of an open API-driven source

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:39.439
for connecting to.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:40.969
And then I have a time series source

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.049
here too, which is a synthetic

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:43.909
sensor that I've set up just sort of

00:03:43.910 --> 00:03:46.039
in my environment to work with.

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:47.299
And the idea is that every one of

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:48.499
these steps, every one of these sort

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.059
of rectangles or nodes you see in

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:52.039
the graph is one step closer

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:54.229
to refining, cleaning, joining,

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.999
applying business logic to that data

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.439
to get us to that semantic

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.729
representation.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.319
So for instance, if I kind of take a

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:01.849
look at one of these datasets, I can

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:03.589
always sort of pop up and say, OK,

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:04.789
what does this data actually look

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:06.619
like? So let's take a look at the

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.509
PySpark code here that

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:09.769
actually shows us how we're actually

00:04:09.770 --> 00:04:11.479
applying logic at this step in the

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.769
pipeline.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:14.509
If I select a different node, I can

00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:15.529
go and take a look at the history

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.539
tab, and I can see that we have full

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.369
data versioning for every

00:04:19.370 --> 00:04:20.719
version of the data that's updated

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.039
in this particular part of the

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:23.929
pipeline. And this is huge because

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:25.279
I can go back and take a look and

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:27.199
say, "OK, who actually updated

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.999
this particular routine or this

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.559
particular schedule of this data

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.699
build?

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.589
What was the actual result for this

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:34.729
version of the data versus the

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:36.079
current version of the data?

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:37.039
And how can you actually roll

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:38.179
forward and backwards through

00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:40.249
different versions of the data with

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:41.839
the code and the metadata at each

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:42.919
point in time?".

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:44.689
So it's a powerful ability for me to

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.369
not only look at data pipelines as

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.199
they exist today, but also be able

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.339
to look at historically how they

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.750
might have changed over time.

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:53.719
There's a few other things I want to

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.129
make sure that we call out here kind

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:57.289
of in this part of the

00:04:57.290 --> 00:04:58.099
Foundry platform.

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.169
So kind of the build system for data

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:01.579
or the data ops part of things.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.159
The first is, you see kind of these

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.139
lines connecting all these different

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:07.819
nodes together, and this is

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:09.679
indication of of lineage

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.479
and. Lineage is actually a concept

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.459
that is intertwined with security

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:14.899
in the Foundry platform.

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:16.759
So we think about how we're securing

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.859
data and how that security will flow

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.419
into the ontology and into

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:22.159
workflows. Beyond that, let's

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.479
actually change the view here for a

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.919
second to take a look.

00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:26.299
So we're going to change here to the

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.249
permissions view and you'll see here

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.559
that it's now showing me dynamically

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:31.939
what my perms---, what my

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:33.499
permissions are across this entire

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:34.309
pipeline.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:35.629
So you'll see it's blue, I have

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:36.769
access to everything here because

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:37.939
I'm sort of the administrator here

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:38.940
of this environment.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.209
But what's actually happening is

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:43.159
we're assigning permissions

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:44.509
to the data sources.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:46.819
So the SAP system, Treasury.gov,

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:48.019
et cetera.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.429
Basically, the role based access

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:50.659
permissions that might be coming in

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:52.699
from an active directory system or

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.319
some sort of other authorization

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.209
scheme that an organization has,

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:57.739
and we're then propagating those

00:05:57.740 --> 00:05:59.269
controls forward.

00:05:59.270 --> 00:06:01.519
So that means that every child

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:03.349
will inherit the union of all the

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:05.329
permissions of its parents

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.259
to make that a little bit less

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.299
esoteric, let's use another example.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:09.829
If I change this permissions view,

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:11.779
which I can do as an admin, to my

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:13.939
colleague Jason Richardson,

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.409
we'll see that Jason doesn't have

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.119
access to most things here.

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:18.139
And that's because he didn't have

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:19.909
access to anything except for the

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.439
Treasury.gov source.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:23.269
And so when the permissions flow,

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.889
he's going to lose access the moment

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:26.479
we start to blend that source with

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.189
other sources.

00:06:28.190 --> 00:06:29.929
You'll notice he actually has one

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:31.909
sort of exceptional piece of access

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.189
here where he should have known that

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:35.089
he would have not had access

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:36.152
originally, which is on this OFAC

00:06:37.250 --> 00:06:38.959
clean data set because we actually

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:41.149
chose to situationally expand

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:43.009
his permissions in this case,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:44.509
but not allow that to sort of

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.609
propagate forward to any other

00:06:46.610 --> 00:06:48.439
pieces of the pipeline.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.909
The point here is is that we can mix

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:51.259
and match very different types of

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:52.819
security models to get very

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.649
granular, robust results

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:56.269
so we can intermingle role based

00:06:56.270 --> 00:06:58.399
access controls, classification

00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.409
based access controls and purpose

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:03.139
based access controls all here.

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:04.429
So we can have very fine grained

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:06.259
control over who is doing what,

00:07:06.260 --> 00:07:07.729
with different personas, different

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:09.109
parts of the organization through

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:10.279
Foundry.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:11.599
And this was very much built from

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:12.799
our own experience in the field as

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.479
Palantir engineers, where if you

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:15.739
thought about just doing security

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:17.839
based off of the location of data

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:19.249
and some sort of file system or some

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.139
sort of bucket location, it became

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.909
really hard to actually make sure

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:24.749
that security was robust as that

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.209
organization organically started to

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:28.669
pull that data into other places.

00:07:28.670 --> 00:07:30.799
So data and security

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.539
kind of flow together with lineage,

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.159
and it's immutable as you work

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:35.299
throughout the rest, throughout the

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.039
entire platform or interact through

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:38.089
the APIs.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:40.039
So that's sort of the first

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:42.269
thing to call out here is security

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.519
is sort of baked right in as part of

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:44.520
the metadata in Foundry.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.909
The second piece I want to call out

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:47.959
here as we kind of work through

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:49.699
things is "OK, so I build out these

00:07:49.700 --> 00:07:50.959
pipelines that get to be quite

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:52.819
robust to refreshing all the time.

00:07:52.820 --> 00:07:54.829
How do I keep these things healthy?"

00:07:54.830 --> 00:07:57.109
And so if I change this to now

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:59.029
a data health view, what we're going

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.189
to see here is this is a view into

00:08:01.190 --> 00:08:02.389
the parts of the pipeline that I've

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.249
instrumented with data quality

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.079
checks, and I can always kind of pop

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.549
this open at the bottom to say,

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:08.899
let's take a look at the checks that

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.279
we have across this part of this

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.329
pipeline.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:13.939
So I'm going to take a look here

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:15.919
at one of these nodes, and we

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:17.539
can take a look and say, let's add a

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:19.189
data health check here.

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:20.809
This is going to pull up a list of

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.339
out-of-the-box health checks that we

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:23.449
can sort of assign to different

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:24.709
points in our pipelines.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:26.929
So is this data updating on time?

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:28.399
Is it refreshing on the schedule

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:30.559
that I expected? Are the files

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.769
the size that I expect?

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.619
And are we seeing the cardinality

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:35.298
of different data columns with the

00:08:35.299 --> 00:08:37.158
statistical results that we expect?

00:08:37.159 --> 00:08:38.089
All of these things can be

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:38.899
quantified here through

00:08:38.900 --> 00:08:41.629
point-and-click, but can also be set

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:43.189
through code as well.

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:44.929
So we'll go in in a second on how we

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:45.784
actually author these
transformations,

00:08:45.785 --> 00:08:47.629
but the data health

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:49.579
component is key to keeping

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:51.349
these pipelines healthy as they're

00:08:51.350 --> 00:08:52.759
sort of refreshing all the time.

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:55.729
And so having these checks right

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:56.989
here saves you having to go to a

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:58.699
different platform to kind of see

00:08:58.700 --> 00:08:59.989
what's going on there before you

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:02.209
kind of come back to this platform

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.259
here.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:04.099
All right.

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.139
So let's now switch back

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:08.119
to our sort of, our default

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.679
view, and let's take a look at more

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.119
detail on what's actually going on

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:12.469
in these individual steps.

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:14.419
So I'm going to open up one of these

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:16.279
sort of, these the steps

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:18.049
of logic here, and we're going to go

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:20.449
in and take a look at the repository

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.009
and how this particular result was

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:23.010
built.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:24.839
So this is going to launch me into

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.339
another application in Foundry

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.169
called Authoring, which is an

00:09:28.170 --> 00:09:29.549
IDE or an integrated development

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:31.679
environment where I can see here

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.029
how I can actually make changes to

00:09:33.030 --> 00:09:34.259
this part of this pipeline with kind

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.689
of full change management built in.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:38.069
So the key thing here is that you'll

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:39.959
see, like we saw in Monocle,

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:41.999
the previous application, there's

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.499
sort of this branch icon here at the

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.389
top. And what this allows us to

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.399
do is treat data

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.169
the way that we treat code.

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:50.849
And so instead of making changes on

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.769
the master branch and trying to then

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.689
propagate my changes through which

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:56.009
could cause all sorts of downstream

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:57.959
issues of not careful, what

00:09:57.960 --> 00:09:59.939
we've actually done is virtualized

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:01.889
the entire change management process

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.539
that would normally involve going to

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.399
a UAT environment, but have done it

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.289
directly here in one instance,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.989
a foundry using logical isolation

00:10:09.990 --> 00:10:11.689
in a cloud-native paradigm.

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:13.589
So what I can do if I want to make

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.419
changes here is I actually open

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:17.309
up a branched version of this

00:10:17.310 --> 00:10:18.659
part of the pipeline.

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.249
And then when I've made my changes

00:10:20.250 --> 00:10:22.139
on that branch, I then create

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.969
a pull request to actually

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.369
merge my changes back into

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:27.509
the master branch.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:28.889
So if we go into some of the old

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:30.119
pull requests, take a look at what

00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.669
these proposed changes look like, we

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:34.469
can actually see line-by-line kind

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.269
of what those changes were.

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:37.709
And we can also get an impact

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:39.719
analysis of, if we were to merge

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:41.399
this into master, what would be

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.289
affected? And because security again

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:44.429
gets blended through different

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:45.569
parent permissions and different

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:47.549
role paradigms, what security

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:48.689
changes would actually occur as

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:49.829
well? And you'd see we didn't

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:51.029
actually see any in this particular

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:52.109
case.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.879
This is a huge difference from the

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:55.499
normal process of saying, OK, if I

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:57.029
wanted to make changes, I'd have to

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:59.069
clone all my pipelines and data over

00:10:59.070 --> 00:11:00.689
to a UAT environment, separate

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:03.209
environment, make some changes

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.189
and then hopefully say a prayer

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.689
and then port them back to master

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:07.619
and hope that everything just sort

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:09.539
of works. Here, we can

00:11:09.540 --> 00:11:11.519
actually do that entire process

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:13.409
with total logical isolation, total

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:14.410
sandboxing, here.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.529
We can iterate on these branched

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.299
versions of the data and actually

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.949
use different kind of analytics

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.899
tools and visualization tools with

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:23.159
that branch data that we'll look at

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.019
later and then we can choose to

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.549
come back and then merge that into

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:27.569
master.

00:11:27.570 --> 00:11:29.099
And the most powerful thing about

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:31.019
this is everything is sort of

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:32.729
git-based in terms of semantics.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:33.989
You'll see here the clone icon at

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:35.729
the top, which means if we ever want

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:37.289
to go back to a prior version of

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.389
master or any of the other branches,

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:41.129
we have a full commit log as well.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:43.049
So we're never, sort of, out

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:44.579
of luck if we merged something in

00:11:44.580 --> 00:11:46.439
that we realized later has to

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:47.639
actually be undone.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.529
We can always just move back like

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.149
we can in Git with code to a

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.199
previous form of the data.

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:54.759
And so being able to treat data like

00:11:54.760 --> 00:11:56.679
code, having tje health checks,

00:11:56.680 --> 00:11:58.239
having the integrated security, we

00:11:58.240 --> 00:11:59.649
think gives you the build system to

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:01.029
be able to go really fast when it

00:12:01.030 --> 00:12:02.709
comes to assembling and growing

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.869
these data pipelines organically,

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.169
which is always the case, right?

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:07.629
These never sort of, sort of,

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.189
waterfall method to building these

00:12:09.190 --> 00:12:10.749
things upfront that never changes.

00:12:10.750 --> 00:12:12.429
It's always a constant sort of state

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:14.289
of iteration and being able to try

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:15.680
to keep up with the latest demands.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:17.799
I want to show you guys one more

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.749
thing before we go into the ontology

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:21.159
and kind of bump things up a level.

00:12:22.180 --> 00:12:23.289
We've talked a lot about being able

00:12:23.290 --> 00:12:24.849
to author kind of data pipelines

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:26.739
here the way that you can actually

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.809
author pipelines and Foundry

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.309
is, you know, by default, we

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:32.229
actually offer two out -of-the-box

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:33.069
compute engines that are

00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:34.059
auto-scaling.

00:12:34.060 --> 00:12:35.739
So this is Flink.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:36.669
We're running on Kubernetes for

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:38.709
streaming computation and spark

00:12:38.710 --> 00:12:39.969
running on Kubernetes for batch

00:12:39.970 --> 00:12:40.959
computation.

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.849
And so you can do any

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:44.439
you can basically use any languages

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.269
that have bindings to those two

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:47.259
runtimes.

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.579
And so if we go back to kind of our

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:49.749
repository here, we'll see where

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:51.609
we're using PySpark, we can

00:12:51.610 --> 00:12:53.829
use Spark sequel, we can use Java,

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:54.879
we can use Scala.

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:57.039
We even have bindings for Groovy.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:00.069
And so we adhere to open standards

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:01.239
all the way through when it comes to

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.099
code authoring and critically when

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:04.689
it comes to data formats as well.

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:05.949
So if you ever want to look at

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:07.089
what's happening with kind of the

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.039
resulting data in any step

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.329
of any sort of transformation in

00:13:10.330 --> 00:13:12.249
Foundry, if we kind of look

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:13.269
at what's happening here in the

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:14.799
preview or we go to the History tab

00:13:14.800 --> 00:13:16.689
here, we'll take a look and we can

00:13:16.690 --> 00:13:18.669
see that any of the data

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:20.499
here is effectively

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:22.479
just data that's in parquet or

00:13:22.480 --> 00:13:24.219
in Avro or in sort of any open

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:25.119
format that I'd expect.

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:27.309
So I can always interoperate easily

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:28.569
with any of the data here in

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:29.529
Foundry.

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:31.059
And critically, even though we're

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:32.529
using Spark and Flink as our

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.359
runtimes, if we take a peek

00:13:34.360 --> 00:13:35.439
at what's actually happening under

00:13:35.440 --> 00:13:36.609
the hood with Foundry's build

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:38.859
service, we'll see it's just calling

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.299
those two runtimes because we've

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:41.949
registered them with Foundry.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:42.819
And so if you have a high

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:44.379
performance computing environment or

00:13:44.380 --> 00:13:45.789
you have something that's an

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:47.199
external compute engine like you're

00:13:47.200 --> 00:13:48.549
using something like another Spark

00:13:48.550 --> 00:13:50.559
environment or a proprietary

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:52.209
runtime, we can register that

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:54.099
runtime with Foundry as an external

00:13:54.100 --> 00:13:54.969
build worker.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:56.629
And when we refresh these pipelines,

00:13:56.630 --> 00:13:57.630
you do the orchestrations, Foundry

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:00.339
will know at certain steps to go and

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.259
federate that computation to that

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:04.419
build worker, which allows

00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:05.499
you again to have a totally

00:14:05.500 --> 00:14:07.779
extensible compute framework here

00:14:07.780 --> 00:14:09.459
with what we think is great starting

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:10.629
defaults. But you're never sort of

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:12.099
locked in to just using the compute

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.600
that we provide as part of Foundry.

00:14:14.940 --> 00:14:16.109
All right.

00:14:16.110 --> 00:14:17.759
So one more thing to show you guys

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:19.229
and then let's bump it up a level.

00:14:19.230 --> 00:14:20.609
So we talked about being able to

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:22.589
compute these pipelines and

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:23.609
author them manually.

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.229
What about data systems that we've

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:26.969
seen over and over again, like the

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.589
SAP's and the other ERPs of the

00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:30.119
world or the CRMs?

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.009
So what we try to do there

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:33.329
is try to say, can we actually

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:34.319
implement what we think of as

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:36.089
software-defined data integrations

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.049
where you don't have to manually

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.419
redo the kind of cleaning

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:41.339
and transforming work that we

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.079
think is actually quite standardized

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:44.549
and possible through kind of

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.679
ML-assisted techniques?

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:47.729
So what I'm going to do is actually

00:14:47.730 --> 00:14:49.679
open up the kind of data source

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:51.119
for this SAP instance we're

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.859
connected to internally.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:54.359
And we're going to take a look here

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:55.859
at kind of how this looks.

00:14:55.860 --> 00:14:58.199
So this is the SAP explorer

00:14:58.200 --> 00:14:59.699
that we have kind of as a kind of a

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.799
rich connection interface here.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:02.909
And you'll see it's showing me kind

00:15:02.910 --> 00:15:05.099
of all the modules in my ERP estate

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:07.679
like my MM, my data dictionaries.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.569
And it's also even pre-populating

00:15:09.570 --> 00:15:11.759
some workflows I might want to do

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.219
typically that are common for SAP.

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:15.749
And the power here is that instead

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:17.069
of having to think about browsing

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:18.479
through all the individual tables

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:20.669
myself, we have a

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:22.229
native connector connecting at the

00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:24.179
application layer, which is called

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:25.829
NetWeaver, and we're actually

00:15:25.830 --> 00:15:27.839
dynamically pulling out all the rich

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:29.699
metadata that actually tells us

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.289
much more than just the raw table

00:15:31.290 --> 00:15:32.969
information in SAP.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:34.169
Like we're able to see all the

00:15:34.170 --> 00:15:36.599
German acronyms auto-translated

00:15:36.600 --> 00:15:37.739
for me. We're able to see kind of

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.929
the foreign keys, the relationships,

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:41.039
and the idea here is that we can

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:42.779
kind of derive out that federated

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:44.549
lineage and then auto build

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.249
pipelines and even starting objects

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:49.379
in a matter of hours, as opposed

00:15:49.380 --> 00:15:50.879
to weeks or months of manually

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:53.369
recreating SAP transformations

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:54.839
on the Foundry side.

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.319
And this applies critically not just

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:58.319
to standard modules like MM

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.209
or SD or DD, but also

00:16:00.210 --> 00:16:01.499
Z tables that you might have

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:02.879
customized. Because we're not just

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.739
doing static mapping, we're doing a

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:06.659
dynamic inference of the metadata

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:07.739
through NetWeaver.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:09.609
And so if you have a very custom SAP

00:16:09.610 --> 00:16:11.339
estate or CRM estate and many people

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:13.379
do, it's very simple to

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:15.299
install the SAP connector, which

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:17.699
is, you know, certified native,

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:19.679
and be able to browse around

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:20.969
and then kind of in a shopping cart

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:22.859
like experience, pull in the data

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:24.959
that's of interest to you into

00:16:24.960 --> 00:16:26.699
a set of pipelines in Foundry that

00:16:26.700 --> 00:16:27.899
you then start to work with.

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:29.309
And actually, that was how we

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:30.989
created these green pipelines here

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.329
that we saw before, and we saw

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:34.499
what was actually produced was

00:16:34.500 --> 00:16:35.519
PySpark.

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:37.709
So even if it gives us a starting

00:16:37.710 --> 00:16:39.479
set of pipelines, we can always

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.219
choose to expand that.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:42.179
We're never sort of given some sort

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:43.769
of black box output that we can't

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:44.669
change.

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:46.499
And so that's an example of,

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:48.449
for more common systems, the

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:50.309
CRMs, the ERPs, the others of

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:51.899
the world, how we're trying to

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.789
invest in speed, time

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:55.709
to value speed for our customers

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:56.849
to say "we don't want you to have to

00:16:56.850 --> 00:16:58.529
redo all those integrations if it's

00:16:58.530 --> 00:16:59.530
not required."

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:01.489
All right.

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:03.199
So we've talked a lot about, in this

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:04.789
first mile, kind of getting data

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:05.989
into the ontology.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:07.098
Now let's actually talk about the

00:17:07.099 --> 00:17:08.568
ontology itself and a little bit

00:17:08.569 --> 00:17:09.529
more depth.

00:17:09.530 --> 00:17:11.479
So I'm going to go now into one

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:12.979
of these objects and kind of pop the

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:14.539
hood on what's actually happening

00:17:14.540 --> 00:17:16.068
here. So to do that, I'm going to

00:17:16.069 --> 00:17:18.289
jump into our ontology management

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:19.309
application.

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:20.629
And this is a view that a data

00:17:20.630 --> 00:17:22.578
governance kind of team

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:24.229
member or a part of the business

00:17:24.230 --> 00:17:25.939
working with it might start to get

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:27.139
involved. Or they might say, "OK,

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:28.849
we've done all the integration work

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:29.899
now let's decide how we're going to

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:31.189
actually make this appear to end

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:32.119
users," right?

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:34.129
So everything from this plant object

00:17:34.130 --> 00:17:35.629
we've been working with, is it a

