step of PySpark based

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:08.139
transformation. I could be using

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:09.039
Pandas.

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:10.757
I can be intermixing R or Spark SQL.

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:13.089
And you know, the joke that we have

00:26:13.090 --> 00:26:14.139
internally is that the most

00:26:14.140 --> 00:26:15.429
interesting thing off the bat to

00:26:15.430 --> 00:26:17.131
data scientists is the import data

00:26:17.132 --> 00:26:18.132
button.

00:26:18.190 --> 00:26:19.179
It's being able to say, "hey, you

00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:21.009
can get clean, consistent access

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:22.599
to that data that you've built into

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.039
the ontology or anywhere else in

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:25.959
Foundry." And as a data scientist,

00:26:25.960 --> 00:26:28.659
I don't spend 80 percent of my time

00:26:28.660 --> 00:26:30.489
rebuilding these data pipelines

00:26:30.490 --> 00:26:31.869
myself. I can just tap into the good

00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:34.149
work of my data ops colleagues

00:26:34.150 --> 00:26:35.919
or my data engineering colleagues.

00:26:35.920 --> 00:26:37.209
And if anything goes wrong with

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:38.589
those data pipelines like the ones

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:40.779
we saw before, I can get immediately

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:42.099
alerted to that because the health

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:43.599
information will flow down to me as

00:26:43.600 --> 00:26:44.379
well.

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:46.119
And so I have a stable, rich,

00:26:46.120 --> 00:26:48.069
healthy foundation to build on, and

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:49.929
I can then kind of use all the

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:51.729
packages and all the techniques that

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:53.859
I am familiar with here to actually

00:26:53.860 --> 00:26:55.719
build out kind of different

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:57.879
models end-to-end batch

00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:59.829
or live inference end points

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:01.389
and then actually plug them directly

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:02.949
into different operational

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:04.269
workflows.

00:27:04.270 --> 00:27:05.829
And of course, I can make use of

00:27:05.830 --> 00:27:07.449
kind of all of the packages I would

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:09.279
expect so we can mirror things

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:10.239
like can't afford you.

00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:11.229
If you have your own package

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:13.059
managers, we have the added

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:14.949
benefit here of kind of

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:16.599
you can set up custom profiles that

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:17.619
not only have different sort of

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.569
compute profiles

00:27:19.570 --> 00:27:21.099
associated with them, but also if

00:27:21.100 --> 00:27:22.689
you have particularly sensitive or

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:24.399
license restricted libraries, you

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:25.749
want only certain users to be able

00:27:25.750 --> 00:27:26.649
to use.

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:28.089
We can enforce that to the same

00:27:28.090 --> 00:27:30.429
security perimeter that's enforcing

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:32.199
data security and ontology security

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:33.639
that we saw before.

00:27:33.640 --> 00:27:34.839
So it allows people to make kind of

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:36.879
secure use of commercial libraries

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:37.880
as well.

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:40.668
And so the idea here is you

00:27:40.669 --> 00:27:41.529
know, build here. We think it's a

00:27:41.530 --> 00:27:42.999
great, streamlined set of tooling,

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:44.889
or you can also connect in

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:45.999
the same way that these tools are

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:47.019
connecting.

00:27:47.020 --> 00:27:48.369
You can connect directly from the

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:50.199
DataRobot to Dataiku, the

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:51.699
SageMakers of the world to the

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:53.679
ontology through open APIs

00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:55.239
or through Python clients that we

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:57.189
provide to all to build your models

00:27:57.190 --> 00:27:59.199
and those environments as well.

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:01.209
And you can connect via REST

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:03.339
via JDBC via direct

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:04.179
file access.

00:28:04.180 --> 00:28:05.499
We have an we have kind of a Foundry

00:28:05.500 --> 00:28:07.389
version of Hadoop FS, and

00:28:07.390 --> 00:28:09.009
it's super streamlined to get data

00:28:09.010 --> 00:28:10.719
sort of in and out of Foundry and

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:12.099
not just data, but also lineage

00:28:12.100 --> 00:28:13.299
information, the semantic

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:15.069
representations, all the versioning

00:28:15.070 --> 00:28:16.489
information, all that's available

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:17.769
through the APIs.

00:28:17.770 --> 00:28:19.479
And so the idea is you build your

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:21.009
models, you've built some here,

00:28:21.010 --> 00:28:22.389
you've built some on other tools.

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:23.829
Now I want to be able to register

00:28:23.830 --> 00:28:25.479
them and use them in a variety of

00:28:25.480 --> 00:28:27.429
different end user applications.

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:28.389
And so what I'm going to do now is

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:30.309
open up my model objectives

00:28:30.310 --> 00:28:31.219
library.

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:32.769
This is kind of mission control for

00:28:32.770 --> 00:28:34.029
all the models that I've been

00:28:34.030 --> 00:28:35.649
working with in this particular

00:28:35.650 --> 00:28:36.609
instance of Foundry that I have

00:28:36.610 --> 00:28:37.869
access to.

00:28:37.870 --> 00:28:39.099
And a lot of this paradigm was

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:40.449
informed by some of our work in the

00:28:40.450 --> 00:28:42.309
defense space where

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:44.259
there's kind of this three part sort

00:28:44.260 --> 00:28:46.659
of approach to deploying operational

00:28:46.660 --> 00:28:48.849
AI. There is, I first want to define

00:28:48.850 --> 00:28:50.109
the modeling problem, right?

00:28:50.110 --> 00:28:50.949
Not actually think about a

00:28:50.950 --> 00:28:51.969
particular model, but what is the

00:28:51.970 --> 00:28:53.709
objective I'm actually trying to run

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:54.609
with here.

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:56.499
I'm probably then testing dozens,

00:28:56.500 --> 00:28:57.939
if not more models against that

00:28:57.940 --> 00:28:58.899
objective.

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:00.159
And then I'm figuring out how I want

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:02.079
to then deploy

00:29:02.080 --> 00:29:03.850
the winning models into production.

00:29:04.880 --> 00:29:07.099
And so I'm going to open up a few of

00:29:07.100 --> 00:29:08.629
the objectives we have here so we

00:29:08.630 --> 00:29:09.769
can take a look at what's actually

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:10.770
going on. And

00:29:11.660 --> 00:29:13.399
the critical thing here is this does

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:14.899
not mean deployed in Foundry

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:16.039
necessarily.

00:29:16.040 --> 00:29:17.479
Several of these models are living

00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:19.499
in other environments in Azure, in

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:21.469
AWS and through managed services

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:22.470
like DataRobot.

00:29:22.880 --> 00:29:24.259
And so we're just registering these

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:26.089
models here, and we're going

00:29:26.090 --> 00:29:27.529
to basically have a holistic

00:29:27.530 --> 00:29:29.479
approach to comparing them as

00:29:29.480 --> 00:29:30.679
we think about which ones are going

00:29:30.680 --> 00:29:32.749
to be deployed into production.

00:29:32.750 --> 00:29:34.489
So if I open up my customer demand

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:36.739
forecast, sort of objective here,

00:29:36.740 --> 00:29:38.329
you'll see at a top level it shows

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:40.219
me all this kind of 360 information

00:29:40.220 --> 00:29:41.309
about the model, right?

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:42.319
Hey, what are we trying to do with

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:43.339
this?

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:44.659
What's the approach kind of broadly

00:29:44.660 --> 00:29:45.589
defined?

00:29:45.590 --> 00:29:46.909
Some information about the latest

00:29:46.910 --> 00:29:48.559
releases, relevant files that

00:29:48.560 --> 00:29:49.789
actually informed or were  used to

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:51.709
train this particular model that

00:29:51.710 --> 00:29:52.909
we're commonly used by every

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:54.739
different model that we tested,

00:29:54.740 --> 00:29:56.149
and even lineage information about

00:29:56.150 --> 00:29:57.289
the applications that are going to

00:29:57.290 --> 00:29:59.089
be depending on this model.

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:00.829
And also, as we'll see a little bit

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:02.749
later on, some information that

00:30:02.750 --> 00:30:04.429
we can about how we're actually

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:05.569
chaining this model with other

00:30:05.570 --> 00:30:07.399
models inside of simulation

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:08.689
environments.

00:30:08.690 --> 00:30:09.739
And it's all of that is kind of

00:30:09.740 --> 00:30:11.689
rolled up here for me and

00:30:11.690 --> 00:30:13.309
I can go in on the sidebar here, for

00:30:13.310 --> 00:30:14.839
instance, take a look and say, let's

00:30:14.840 --> 00:30:15.919
take a look at all the models that

00:30:15.920 --> 00:30:17.869
we've actually proposed to be

00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:19.429
kind of deployed against this

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:20.209
objective right now.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:21.619
See, some from my colleague Matt,

00:30:21.620 --> 00:30:23.449
some from my colleague Andrew, and I

00:30:23.450 --> 00:30:24.709
can always look back in time to say,

00:30:24.710 --> 00:30:26.072
OK, for like this lasso model, we

00:30:27.260 --> 00:30:28.489
actually can see the details about

00:30:28.490 --> 00:30:30.379
where it was built, when it was

00:30:30.380 --> 00:30:32.329
built, and also if it was rejected,

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:33.469
why that was.

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:34.909
And so kind of the full kind of life

00:30:34.910 --> 00:30:36.709
cycle around kind of the approval

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:38.569
process like we saw with data,

00:30:38.570 --> 00:30:39.570
how we're treating data

00:30:40.970 --> 00:30:42.229
----- like code. We're also kind of

00:30:42.230 --> 00:30:43.909
treating models like code in kind of

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:45.349
the same way the metadata around the

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:46.350
approval process.

00:30:47.750 --> 00:30:48.859
And, you know, if I kind of switch

00:30:48.860 --> 00:30:49.999
over to another objective here,

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:51.289
which might be my sort of campaign

00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:53.029
model, I can even go into the

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:54.619
comparisons tab here and take a look

00:30:54.620 --> 00:30:56.269
and say, OK, so for different models

00:30:56.270 --> 00:30:57.619
that we're assessing, we rejected

00:30:57.620 --> 00:30:58.969
one of them, but let's still add it

00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:00.169
into the comparison.

00:31:00.170 --> 00:31:01.699
We have a random forest model and

00:31:01.700 --> 00:31:03.169
SVM model and then your regression

00:31:03.170 --> 00:31:05.029
model, and I want to I compare kind

00:31:05.030 --> 00:31:06.259
of the key theories, the key

00:31:06.260 --> 00:31:07.789
