00:34:20.429 --> 00:34:21.388
So I'm going to filter here on the

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:23.009
left hand side, and I'm going to

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:24.479
take a look at one of these alerts

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:25.408
here, which is a business

00:34:25.409 --> 00:34:27.209
interruption related to COVID.

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:28.229
And you'll see that this is pulling

00:34:28.230 --> 00:34:29.249
together lots of different

00:34:29.250 --> 00:34:30.479
information from everything we've

00:34:30.480 --> 00:34:31.738
covered so far.

00:34:31.739 --> 00:34:33.509
Right. This alert was generated

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:35.579
maybe by a model pipeline.

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:37.079
It is pulling in data from the

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:38.638
supplier, and the plants are

00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:40.769
different links in my ontology.

00:34:40.770 --> 00:34:42.419
It's showing me the impacted plant

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:44.428
that this pertains to, and it's

00:34:44.429 --> 00:34:45.839
actually showing you recommended

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:47.698
actions as well, which are in this

00:34:47.699 --> 00:34:48.959
case coming in from a model that

00:34:48.960 --> 00:34:50.189
I've built.

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:52.319
The critical thing here

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:53.428
is we're not just stopping with

00:34:53.429 --> 00:34:55.109
looking at this alert and saying,

00:34:55.110 --> 00:34:56.279
"well, OK, looks like we have a

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:57.959
problem here." We're saying we want

00:34:57.960 --> 00:34:59.399
to be able to take action now to

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:00.308
actually do something in response.

00:35:00.309 --> 00:35:02.099
And you'll see that we have

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:03.389
different actions that we've

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:04.919
actually pre-assembled for these

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:06.739
users canceling an order

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:08.339
or partially fulfilling an order or

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:09.539
actually going into more advanced

00:35:09.540 --> 00:35:11.519
actions like reallocation that will

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:13.499
look at in just a second.

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:14.939
But if I hit this cancel customer

00:35:14.940 --> 00:35:16.499
order button, you'll see that it

00:35:16.500 --> 00:35:18.509
allows me now to sort of it kind of

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:20.279
preconfigured and action I can take

00:35:20.280 --> 00:35:21.839
here on this particular alert or a

00:35:21.840 --> 00:35:23.909
related alert, and I can then choose

00:35:23.910 --> 00:35:25.739
to submit this in a way that

00:35:25.740 --> 00:35:27.329
will be nondestructively captured

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:28.949
back into the ontology.

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:30.239
And you might be asking, well, how

00:35:30.240 --> 00:35:31.169
does this work, right?

00:35:31.170 --> 00:35:32.669
I don't want any user to be able to

00:35:32.670 --> 00:35:34.529
take any action on any part

00:35:34.530 --> 00:35:36.449
of the ontology and start to update

00:35:36.450 --> 00:35:37.559
things right. That would be chaos.

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:38.819
We can't have people modifying

00:35:38.820 --> 00:35:39.840
values willy nilly.

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:42.659
Turns out, the way that actions work

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:43.979
is they're actually part of the

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:46.049
ontology at the core level.

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.119
So if we go back to the ontology

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:49.559
configuration interface that we saw

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:51.179
before we were talking about

00:35:51.180 --> 00:35:52.469
bringing our plant together and all

00:35:52.470 --> 00:35:54.239
of the connections here, if we go

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:55.649
back to kind of the main page, we'll

00:35:55.650 --> 00:35:57.659
see their object types, of course,

00:35:57.660 --> 00:35:58.619
that we're defining.

00:35:58.620 --> 00:35:59.879
There are link types which we've

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:01.589
seen all throughout, but the

00:36:01.590 --> 00:36:03.749
critical third element is actions

00:36:03.750 --> 00:36:05.489
as well. And this is the dynamism

00:36:05.490 --> 00:36:07.469
here that kind of makes the ontology

00:36:07.470 --> 00:36:09.389
truly special is we're not

00:36:09.390 --> 00:36:10.379
just talking about kind of

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:12.029
semantics, we're also talking about

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:13.019
kinetics.

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:14.159
We're talking about the kind of

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:16.079
actions that can modify the

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:18.389
state of the ontology in a compliant

00:36:18.390 --> 00:36:19.439
manner.

00:36:19.440 --> 00:36:20.849
And so if I, for instance, search

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:23.129
for that cancel customer order

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:25.079
action that we saw before, we'll see

00:36:25.080 --> 00:36:27.389
this is in our library of actions.

00:36:27.390 --> 00:36:28.769
And if I open this up, you'll see

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.239
all sorts of related information

00:36:30.240 --> 00:36:31.529
like we have with other parts of the

00:36:31.530 --> 00:36:33.719
ontology, the API

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:35.489
name for this, this cancel custom

00:36:35.490 --> 00:36:37.379
order, the actual ID that we can

00:36:37.380 --> 00:36:38.609
use, we can programmatically

00:36:38.610 --> 00:36:40.349
interact with this and the actual

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:42.299
logic used to create this particular

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:44.309
cancel customer order action.

00:36:44.310 --> 00:36:45.310
So we see here

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:48.329
what objects that pertains to, what

00:36:48.330 --> 00:36:50.279
properties it will be updating,

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:52.229
what parameters it takes and even

00:36:52.230 --> 00:36:53.880
what validations it uses as well.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:56.429
And this is all kind of configured

00:36:56.430 --> 00:36:57.509
here through point-and-click, but it

00:36:57.510 --> 00:36:59.039
could be configured through a much

00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:00.149
more kind of robust kind of

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:02.309
code-based interface as well,

00:37:02.310 --> 00:37:03.959
using the authoring tools that we

00:37:03.960 --> 00:37:05.939
saw before here

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.259
and kind of the more data ops part

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:08.260
of the stack.

00:37:08.970 --> 00:37:11.159
And so with this action essentially

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:13.259
defined every time I want to cancel

00:37:13.260 --> 00:37:15.239
a customer order, I can do so

00:37:15.240 --> 00:37:17.549
in a reliable, repeatable, auditable

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:18.359
way.

00:37:18.360 --> 00:37:19.739
And so that means that every

00:37:19.740 --> 00:37:20.909
application builder isn't

00:37:20.910 --> 00:37:22.589
implementing their own version of

00:37:22.590 --> 00:37:23.789
what it means to cancel a custom

00:37:23.790 --> 00:37:25.679
order. It means they simply use

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:27.659
this action of the library, and

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:29.459
it knows intelligently how to

00:37:29.460 --> 00:37:32.489
basically modify different objects.

00:37:32.490 --> 00:37:34.439
It knows what sort of precautions

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:35.459
to take. So if I'm a junior

00:37:35.460 --> 00:37:37.349
employee, it might be that when

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:38.789
I click the cancel customer order,

00:37:38.790 --> 00:37:40.709
it's aware of who I am and it has

00:37:40.710 --> 00:37:42.299
to go and then get approval from my

00:37:42.300 --> 00:37:44.519
manager before it actually

00:37:44.520 --> 00:37:45.659
gets confirmed.

00:37:45.660 --> 00:37:47.519
And then it might be that the last

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:48.959
part of this is a webhook or some

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:50.639
sort of write-back routine that then

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:52.559
actually synchronizes this change

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:54.419
back to an ERP or back to

00:37:54.420 --> 00:37:55.709
an MES system.

00:37:55.710 --> 00:37:56.909
Again, all of that is a pretty

00:37:56.910 --> 00:37:58.409
complex flow for an action or a

00:37:58.410 --> 00:38:00.119
piece of logic that I don't want the

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:01.409
application builder to have to think

00:38:01.410 --> 00:38:02.669
about. I just want them to be able

00:38:02.670 --> 00:38:04.146
to embed that piece of logic here as

00:38:04.147 --> 00:38:06.179
an action and have Foundry

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:07.319
at a system level be able to deal

00:38:07.320 --> 00:38:08.579
with all that orchestration that

00:38:08.580 --> 00:38:09.580
happens thereafter.

00:38:11.080 --> 00:38:12.969
So we've kind of alluded to this,

00:38:12.970 --> 00:38:14.019
but what this means for the

00:38:14.020 --> 00:38:15.909
application builder is that even

00:38:15.910 --> 00:38:17.019
though these applications we've been

00:38:17.020 --> 00:38:18.579
looking at here in this particular

00:38:18.580 --> 00:38:20.349
supply chain control tower example

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:22.299
looks very custom - It's

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:24.219
actually all just built using a

00:38:24.220 --> 00:38:25.629
low code application builder, and in

00:38:25.630 --> 00:38:26.979
this case, a no code application

00:38:26.980 --> 00:38:28.389
builder. And if I want to add

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:29.469
something here to the top, for

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:31.089
instance, it's very simple, just a

00:38:31.090 --> 00:38:33.249
point-and-click my way into adding

00:38:33.250 --> 00:38:35.349
a new widget here in

00:38:35.350 --> 00:38:36.819
this particular view, so I could add

00:38:36.820 --> 00:38:38.529
an object table, for instance.

00:38:38.530 --> 00:38:39.879
And it's super simple to start to

00:38:39.880 --> 00:38:41.919
wire in different things

00:38:41.920 --> 00:38:42.849
into these views, right?

00:38:42.850 --> 00:38:44.589
Because I'm not dealing with pulling

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:46.479
data into kind of a

00:38:46.480 --> 00:38:48.219
kind of siloed database and trying

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:49.539
to do kind of things in a one-off

00:38:49.540 --> 00:38:51.459
manner. I'm connected directly to

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:53.649
the ontology and so I can easily

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:55.809
open up a set of objects

00:38:55.810 --> 00:38:57.129
you've been working with before,

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:58.959
like the plants, and I can

00:38:58.960 --> 00:39:01.329
start to actually pull them in here

00:39:01.330 --> 00:39:02.769
and take a look at how they will

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:04.689
actually get bound to these

00:39:04.690 --> 00:39:06.399
widgets that we've set up.

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:08.829
And so here, I'll type in our MSC

00:39:08.830 --> 00:39:10.689
plant in this case, and

00:39:10.690 --> 00:39:12.099
it will now be able to pull in all

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:13.809
that data in a way that is aware of

00:39:13.810 --> 00:39:16.239
who I am and my security profile.

00:39:16.240 --> 00:39:17.739
I can add all the properties here,

00:39:17.740 --> 00:39:19.539
and just like that, I've added in a

00:39:19.540 --> 00:39:21.489
new widget in the same

00:39:21.490 --> 00:39:22.659
way that I can add in data.

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:25.179
I can add in those actions as well.

00:39:25.180 --> 00:39:26.559
And what this means is, as an

00:39:26.560 --> 00:39:28.479
application developer, I no longer

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:29.799
have to think about the full stack

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:31.689
of considerations myself every time

00:39:31.690 --> 00:39:33.579
I want to deploy a new operational

00:39:33.580 --> 00:39:35.109
workflow or application.

00:39:35.110 --> 00:39:36.249
I don't have to think about setting

00:39:36.250 --> 00:39:37.929
up a new hosting environment.

00:39:37.930 --> 00:39:39.669
The databases, the indexing, the

00:39:39.670 --> 00:39:41.469
compute, the storage, that's maybe

00:39:41.470 --> 00:39:42.470
non-database.

00:39:42.670 --> 00:39:44.649
How am I capturing back feedback

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:46.989
from users? How am I binding models

00:39:46.990 --> 00:39:48.459
to the different application states?

00:39:48.460 --> 00:39:49.719
How am I capturing feedback from

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:50.649
those models?

00:39:50.650 --> 00:39:52.569
All of that is taken care of below

00:39:52.570 --> 00:39:54.549
the level of the application because

00:39:54.550 --> 00:39:56.019
of what we saw in the data

00:39:56.020 --> 00:39:57.849
integration, in the set up of the

00:39:57.850 --> 00:39:59.859
ontology, and the actions in

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:02.049
the actual wiring in of the models

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:03.189
that come together in these

00:40:03.190 --> 00:40:05.109
objectives and the actual binding

00:40:05.110 --> 00:40:06.819
of the models to the ontology, to

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:08.559
these objectives as well, which

00:40:08.560 --> 00:40:10.179
means you can do a fully feature

00:40:10.180 --> 00:40:12.519
rich compound applications

00:40:12.520 --> 00:40:14.439
in a couple of hours or

00:40:14.440 --> 00:40:16.329
a day or two, as opposed to

00:40:16.330 --> 00:40:17.649
spending months or quarters of

00:40:17.650 --> 00:40:18.650
effort.

00:40:19.850 --> 00:40:21.169
So we think this is a really

00:40:21.170 --> 00:40:22.789
powerful part of the platform, and

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:24.199
oftentimes, you know, this is this

00:40:24.200 --> 00:40:25.579
is just, you know, an example of

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:27.379
kind of where the kind of

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:28.729
operational capability gets

00:40:28.730 --> 00:40:29.929
surfaced.

00:40:29.930 --> 00:40:30.889
Kind of what we've been looking at

00:40:30.890 --> 00:40:32.809
here is one example

00:40:32.810 --> 00:40:34.219
of an operational application

00:40:34.220 --> 00:40:35.839
builder called Workshop.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:37.279
There are other application builders

00:40:37.280 --> 00:40:38.299
in Foundry as well.

00:40:38.300 --> 00:40:39.589
So there's another one called Slate

00:40:39.590 --> 00:40:41.419
as sort of a sibling application.

00:40:41.420 --> 00:40:42.769
And the key thing here is, of

00:40:42.770 --> 00:40:44.509
course, you can go and you can build

00:40:44.510 --> 00:40:46.279
your own custom applications as

00:40:46.280 --> 00:40:48.109
well, and you can kind of do it an

00:40:48.110 --> 00:40:50.059
object oriented manner or even do

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:51.709
it in a tabular manner or a more

00:40:51.710 --> 00:40:53.539
classical manner as well.

00:40:53.540 --> 00:40:54.559
There's kind of a full set of

00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:56.179
documentation here around not just

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:57.619
sort of interacting with existing

00:40:57.620 --> 00:40:59.479
applications, but building your

00:40:59.480 --> 00:41:01.909
own applications, using the APIs

00:41:01.910 --> 00:41:03.469
and Foundry using every part of what

00:41:03.470 --> 00:41:04.429
we've talked about kind of

00:41:04.430 --> 00:41:05.430
throughout this demo.

00:41:06.810 --> 00:41:07.799
Awesome.

00:41:07.800 --> 00:41:09.269
Now I have one last thing to show

00:41:09.270 --> 00:41:10.049
you guys.

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:11.399
And then then we'll sign off here

00:41:11.400 --> 00:41:13.349
for this particular demo,

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:14.759
and that is kind of how these things

00:41:14.760 --> 00:41:16.649
start to come together in

00:41:16.650 --> 00:41:18.119
kind of a more strategic kind of

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:19.799
digital twin paradigm.

00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:22.929
So what am I open up here is

00:41:22.930 --> 00:41:24.429
one final application here in

00:41:24.430 --> 00:41:26.169
Foundry that we call Vertex.

00:41:26.170 --> 00:41:27.609
Vertex is kind of like the map

00:41:27.610 --> 00:41:28.959
application in Foundry.

00:41:28.960 --> 00:41:30.359
Sorry, the graph application in

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:31.449
Foundry you might think of, right?

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:32.349
It's showing me kind of all the

00:41:32.350 --> 00:41:34.299
relationships here, kind

00:41:34.300 --> 00:41:35.709
of between all my different objects,

00:41:35.710 --> 00:41:36.759
right? Like if you were just to look

00:41:36.760 --> 00:41:37.689
at, we started here, you might think

00:41:37.690 --> 00:41:38.649
this looks kind of nuts, right?

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:40.629
There's so much going on here,

00:41:40.630 --> 00:41:42.099
but now we know exactly what this

00:41:42.100 --> 00:41:43.809
is, right? These are this is just

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:45.249
showing me kind of the kind of

00:41:45.250 --> 00:41:46.629
connection that I've built into my

00:41:46.630 --> 00:41:47.919
ontology, right, where every one of

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:49.959
these object types has a lot of data

00:41:49.960 --> 00:41:51.069
being integrated into it.

00:41:51.070 --> 00:41:52.599
And then we've also overlaid the

00:41:52.600 --> 00:41:53.829
different machine learning models

00:41:53.830 --> 00:41:55.629
and data science approaches as well.

00:41:55.630 --> 00:41:57.669
So in this case, this is an example

00:41:57.670 --> 00:41:59.619
that's based off of

00:41:59.620 --> 00:42:00.939
one of our medical supplier

00:42:00.940 --> 00:42:03.009
customers, kind of loosely based

00:42:03.010 --> 00:42:04.839
off of anonymized, of course,

00:42:04.840 --> 00:42:06.429
from the work they did during COVID

00:42:06.430 --> 00:42:08.319
kind of the early phases of COVID,

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:09.399
where they needed to really map out

00:42:09.400 --> 00:42:10.869
kind of the full flow of how they

00:42:10.870 --> 00:42:11.799
were building and shipping

00:42:11.800 --> 00:42:13.689
ventilators from everything

00:42:13.690 --> 00:42:14.889
from their upstream suppliers to

00:42:14.890 --> 00:42:16.809
intermediate goods to their plants

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:18.609
all the way down to their actual

00:42:18.610 --> 00:42:20.409
distribution centers and their end

00:42:20.410 --> 00:42:22.359
customers themselves.

00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:23.529
And so what this is showing here is

00:42:23.530 --> 00:42:24.999
sort of the bird's eye view of the

00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:26.319
entire value chain, right?

00:42:26.320 --> 00:42:27.699
And being able to kind of scrub

00:42:27.700 --> 00:42:29.019
backwards and forwards in time and

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:30.729
saying, show me where alerts are

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:32.169
firing, where inventory is getting

00:42:32.170 --> 00:42:34.029
to dangerous levels, where we're

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:35.589
basically experiencing kind of

00:42:35.590 --> 00:42:37.539
fundamental dislocations, right?

00:42:37.540 --> 00:42:38.529
And I can always kind of go

00:42:38.530 --> 00:42:39.939
backwards and forwards kind of scrub

00:42:39.940 --> 00:42:41.799
through kind of different points in

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:43.179
time here to be able to look at kind

00:42:43.180 --> 00:42:44.979
of where things were in kind of the

00:42:44.980 --> 00:42:46.509
danger zone at different points in

00:42:46.510 --> 00:42:47.759
time.

00:42:47.760 --> 00:42:49.139
And so that's useful, of course, to

00:42:49.140 --> 00:42:50.159
be able to kind of be able to get

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:52.109
visibility into the whole kind

00:42:52.110 --> 00:42:53.519
of connected chain here, which was

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:55.049
spread across dozens of different

00:42:55.050 --> 00:42:57.059
